Z||000002||Raydar||12-06-2000||06:15 PM||||You may be right, but usually, if the oil didn't help then it's a valve that's leaking. Was the engine smoking or running poorly? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||12-06-2000||06:49 PM||||engines runs find, but ever since i got it it smoked a little more than i thought it should from the exhaust. some1 told me it might be the valve seals but i never checked them.<BR>when i started my car up after putting the oil in, a huge cloud of smoke came out of the exhaust. is that normal? also it was hesitating a little bit when i pushed the throttle.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||12-07-2000||08:39 AM||||I'm definitely no Duke expert, but think those compression readings sound good enough.  Wouldn't go for a rebuild (especially if you're kind of lazy like me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>Try replacing the valve seals <B>first</B>, especially if you get excessive smoke on startup.  Much cheaper/ less downtime than engine rebuild.<P>Should the engine smoke a lot after you squirt oil in the cylinders?  Absolutely, at least until the oil gets burned out of there. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||12-07-2000||06:20 PM||||Replace the valve seals. I have done this to many Iron Dukes. It seems that the valve seals in these things go easily. If your engine gives a blue puff of smoke every time you start it up in the morning than the valve guide seals are shot and they usually are. As for the compression if they are within 10% of each other than the engine is OK, which they are barely. <P>So you want to burn me huh!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-07-2000).]||||reg||1
